{
  "image_name": "00e16ac38fe03c05d103f90b4821c445.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure illustrates how stressed or dying tumor cells can drive an anti-tumor immune response through dendritic cells and T cells. The tumor mass is shown releasing different danger signals as cells undergo autophagy, stress, and death. First, ATP released from the tumor appears to attract immature dendritic cells into the tumor area through purinergic signaling, promoting pre-DC recruitment. As tumor cells become stressed and die, they expose or release additional molecules, including ANXA1, calreticulin, and HMGB1, which help guide dendritic cells, support immunological synapse formation, and stimulate phagocytosis of dying tumor material.\n\nThe dendritic cells then take up tumor antigens and mature, with labels indicating receptors such as FPR1, CD91, and TLR involved in sensing these signals. This maturation enables antigen presentation, after which activated dendritic cells prime T cells. The final part of the figure shows cytokines such as CXCL10, CXCR5-related signals, and IFN contributing to T-cell recruitment and amplification of the immune response around the tumor.\n\nOverall, the image presents a coordinated sequence in which immunogenic tumor cell death converts local stress signals into dendritic-cell activation and, ultimately, T-cell-mediated anti-tumor immunity.",
